## Development Flow

1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `vision.md`, and `architecture.md`.
2. Select or create the relevant `features/<feature-slug>/` folder (from `features/_template/`).
3. Record the modifying prompt in `features/<feature-slug>/feature.md`.
4. Update requirements, acceptance criteria, and open questions.
5. Add or update ADRs for architectural choices.
6. Implement only what the documents specify.
7. Validate with focused tests or checks (`make test`, `make lint`, `make typecheck`).
8. Record implementation and verification notes in the feature document.

## Conventions

- Python style, formatting, and code-change behavior are defined in `AGENTS.md`.
- Lint with ruff and type-check with pyright; both must stay clean.
- Test with pytest (`pytest-django`); keep tests close to the app they cover.
- Run the project through the `Makefile` targets (`make up`, `make migrate`, `make test`, …).
- Configuration is read from the environment via `python-decouple`; never commit secrets.

## Feature Folder Convention

Each feature folder contains:

- `feature.md`: current normative state — purpose, requirements, acceptance criteria, constraints.
- `SKILL.md`: instructions the AI coding agent follows when changing the feature; discoverable via `.agents/skills/` (Codex) and `.claude/skills/` (Claude Code).
- `history.md`: append-only prompt log, implementation summaries, and verification results.
- `adr/`: feature-scoped architecture decision records.

Create a new feature with `make new-feature name=<slug>`, which copies `_template/`
and adds both skill symlinks.

## ADR Convention

Use an ADR when a choice has durable consequences: runtime/framework/library/storage
choices, data-model or API-contract decisions, security or privacy decisions,
cross-feature abstractions, or significant tradeoffs. Each ADR records Title, Status,
Date, Context, Decision, Consequences, and Alternatives Considered.

